<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zadar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zara, Iader, Jadera</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zadar is a city on the Adriatic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, known in Italian as Zara. A Roman colony as Iader, it was later a leading Dalmatian city contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> sacked it for Venice in 1202. Ruled by Venice from 1409 to 1797, it then served as capital of the Austrian Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, and was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from 1920 to 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zadar.jpg" alt="Zadar" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: dronepicr · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Forum_in_Zadar,_Croatia_(48607823862).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Zadar is the oldest continuously inhabited city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. It lies in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> on the Adriatic Sea, facing the islands of Ugljan and Pašman across the narrow Zadar Strait. The old city stands on a promontory that was once separated from the mainland by a deep moat, since filled in, and the harbour to the north-east is safe and spacious. Zadar was a Liburnian settlement laid out in the 9th century BCE and called Iader. By the 7th century BCE it was an important centre of trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, and it became the main seat of the Liburnian sea power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>It became a Roman municipium in 59 BCE and a colony in 48 BCE, with a regular street grid, a forum and a capitolium. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> in 476 CE it became the capital of the Byzantine Theme of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>. It was briefly under Frankish rule at the start of the 9th century, and in 998 it sought the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> against Neretvian pirates and swore allegiance to Doge Pietro II Orseolo. Control passed repeatedly between Venice and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>. In 1202 the Venetians, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>, reconquered and plundered the city, and in 1358 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zadar">Treaty of Zadar</a> gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>.</p><p>On 31 July 1409 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-of-naples">Ladislaus of Naples</a> sold Zadar and his dynasty's rights to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> for 100,000 ducats. It became the de facto capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetian-dalmatia">Venetian Dalmatia</a> and, once the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> had conquered the hinterland, a stronghold protecting Venetian trade. Between the 15th and 17th centuries it was an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> centre, producing the sculptor Giorgio da Sebenico and Giovanni Francesco Fortunio, who wrote the first Italian grammar, and Croatian writers such as Petar Zoranić and Šime Budinić.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> in 1797 it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and from 1815 to 1918 it was capital of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>. In the 19th century it was a centre of the Illyrian movement, amid growing polarisation between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and Dalmatian Italians, who then formed a majority of its citizens. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> took it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and the Partisans captured it on 1 November 1944. In 1947 it became part of the Socialist Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. About 10,000 Italians left, and the last Italian schools closed in 1953.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Zadar is the seat of Zadar County and the northern Dalmatian region, and the fifth-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. The University of Zadar traces its origin to a Dominican institution of 1396, which closed in 1807 and was re-established in 1956. The Roman forum, parts of the aqueduct and the medieval churches survive, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> included its fortifications in the Venetian Works of Defence in 2017.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zadar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Fourth Crusade the Venetians transported the crusading army, diverted it to <b>Zara</b> in 1202 and to Constantinople, which fell in 1204; Doge Enrico Dandolo secured Venice a claim to three-eighths of the Byzantine Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Singers outside Armenia have taken it up as well, among them the Canadian Isabel Bayrakdarian, the French singers Hélène Segara and Patrick Fiori, and the Russian singer <b>Zara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dle-yaman">Dle Yaman</a></li><li><blockquote>Crusaders unable to pay the Venetians for their fleet arrived at <b>Zara</b> on the Dalmatian coast and besieged it for Venice.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-10">10 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Italy received Istria, <b>Zara</b> and several islands, the rest of Dalmatia went to the Yugoslav kingdom, and Fiume became a free state.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-12">12 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its sultans, based at <b>Zara</b>, emerged as an independent local power around 1800, after the authority of the Qasimid State in the south had faded.</blockquote><a href="/state/audhali-sultanate">Audhali Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Dukes ruled from seats at Nin, Klis and Bijaći, with a Latin-influenced Christian elite and Byzantine Dalmatian towns such as <b>Zadar</b> and Split nearby.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-croatia">Duchy of Croatia</a></li></ul></section>
